2.00 (unreleased)

  - Router::Right->new(legacy => 1) keeps the behaviour of 1.05 for
    routes written for it: the resource routes and the method rule
    described below (also legacy_resources and legacy_methods, each on
    its own), and a warning rather than an error for a route that can
    never match.

  - match() gives a request to the first route whose path matches and
    which takes its method, as most routers do. Before, the first path to
    match decided: if its routes refused the method the result was a 405,
    though a later route took it. A 405 now means no route with that
    path takes the method, and allowed_methods() then lists the methods
    of all of them. A route restricted to POST no longer keeps a GET from
    a wider route defined after it. Router::Right->new(legacy_methods
    => 1) keeps the old rule.

  - A route or scope that names its own methods no longer adds its
    scope's to them: with('POST /a')->add('GET /b') takes GET alone.

  - add() refuses a route that could never match, because an earlier
    route with the same path takes all of its methods and has a
    different payload. With the same payload it is a second name.

  - resource() takes format, a pattern for the format (so an id may hold
    a dot) or a false value for routes with no format.

  - match() looks a path with no placeholder up in a hash rather than the
    regex, and reads only the matched route's captures. url() builds its
    query string itself and parses the URL once, skips escaping a value
    that needs none, and checks a placeholder with the default pattern
    without a regex. Methods that take the same paths, as GET and HEAD
    usually do, share one compiled regex. Tie::IxHash is no longer
    needed.

  - URI::QueryParam is no longer loaded. Code that calls query_param()
    on a URI must load it itself.

  - A placeholder in a route's parsed path ({route} of routes() and
    Match->route) holds pname, check and format; optional, type and
    regex are gone.

  - resource() builds seven routes, not eleven. The formatted_ twins are
    gone (the {.format} routes beside them always matched first), update
    takes PATCH as well as PUT, and an edit path ends in its format
    (/messages/{id}/edit{.format}, not /messages/{id}{.format}/edit).
    Every route name starts with the resource: message_new and
    message_edit, not new_message and edit_message.
    Router::Right->new(legacy_resources => 1) keeps the old routes.

  - resource() takes only and except to choose its actions; member and
    collection for further routes ('POST take' adds /bookings/{id}/take
    as booking_take, GET alone when no method is given); id to constrain
    the id; actions to add payload to one action; and plural to name the
    collection. A string passed as collection still names it.

  - with() returns a Router::Right, not a Router::Right::Submapper. A
    scope is the router's own class (so a subclass's overrides apply to
    routes added through it) sharing the same routes, with a name prefix,
    a path prefix and default options. Router::Right::Submapper and
    _submapper_class() are gone; code that subclassed the submapper
    should override the method on its router subclass instead.

  - A payload hash passed to add() or with() is never modified.

  - match() works on Perl 5.10 to 5.16. It recorded the matching route
    through a closure inside the regex, which those versions do not bind,
    so every path matched the first route defined.

  - url() checks each placeholder value against its whole pattern;
    {id:\d+} no longer accepts 'abc1', and {id} refuses a slash.

  - url() percent-encodes placeholder values as UTF-8. '?', '#', '%',
    spaces and non-ASCII characters were passed through or encoded as
    Latin-1, so the URL matched a different value or none. A caller that
    escaped values itself now gets them escaped twice. The escaping is
    done here rather than by URI::Escape, whose older releases mangle a
    custom character class.

  - url() leaves out an optional placeholder passed as undef or ''.

  - A placeholder pattern may contain balanced braces ({code:\d{3}}).
    A pattern that does not compile, an unbalanced brace, or a placeholder
    name that is not an identifier is refused when the route is added.

  - Router::Right::Match has real accessors for the route fields. Any
    other method name dies instead of returning undef, and can() answers
    for the fields.

  - A route may be named 0.

  - routes() lists the defined routes; _resolve_param() lets a subclass
    turn a placeholder value into a string before url() checks it.

1.05 Feb 12, 2024

  - IMPORTANT! Note potential non-backwards compatible change:

  - The match() method now returns a blessed match instance.

    Under the hood, it is still a hash reference and still only contains
    the payload contents, so as long as your code does not test whether
    the payload is blessed, it should continue to work as before.

    To revert to the old behavior, create a Router::Right subclass and
    override the _match_class() method to return the undefined value.

  - Tie::IxHash used in url() method to maintain ordering of parameters
    as passed in.

0.04

  - Fix nested resource()

0.03

  - Add resource() support

0.01

  - Initial release
